sum='The poet meets a funny kind of man walking down the street. He does everything the wrong way round: he wears a shoe upon his head and hats upon his feet. He politely raises the shoe and smiles – a “funny sounding sight”. He says, “Allow me to present Your Highness with a rose,” but takes out a currant bun and holds it to the poet’s nose. The poet staggers back and says she never saw a rose with such a “funny looking smell”. Then he sits on the ground and sings – a “funny feeling sound”. When the poet asks, “My friend, why do you wear two hats upon your feet?”, he does not answer; he turns the other way about and hops home on his head. The poem is a humorous nonsense poem. The poet mixes up the senses (sounding sight, looking smell, feeling sound) to match the man’s topsy-turvy behaviour. Rhyme scheme: ABCB.',
