  'IV. 1. humorous and nonsensical  2. dialogue  3. alliteration',
  'V. 1. But I had never seen such a funny sounding sight before.',
  '2. The man mixes everything up, so the poet also mixes up the senses – a sight cannot “sound” and a sound cannot be “felt”. The wrong pairings match his topsy-turvy behaviour and create humour.',
  'VI. Clowns in a circus, street performers, mimicry artists, fancy-dress shows, comedy shows and cartoon characters behave like this for fun.',
  '1. Humour comes from illogical actions: (a) a shoe on his head and hats on his feet; (b) he offers a “rose” but gives a currant bun; (c) he hops home on his head.',
  '2. “Funny” is repeated in every stanza (funny kind of man, funny sounding sight, funny looking smell, funny feeling sound). It ties the poem together and keeps the tone light, telling us to simply enjoy the nonsense.',
